                <pb facs="0142" n="80" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg80"/>

                <head>No. 65.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Williamson to Mr. Evarts</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States at Central America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Punta Arenas</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1878-09-02"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >September</hi> 2, 1878</date>. (Rec’d Sept. 16.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 777.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to state that, having
                    touched at several Pacific ports on my way from Guatemala to this place, and
                    having gathered information in respect to public affairs in Central America from
                    sources considered reliable, I am able to report that all the States are at
                    peace, and that there is no immediate prospect of disturbance from war or
                    revolution. The crops are reported to be unusually promising for this season of
                    the year, and it is expected the yield of coffee will be larger than it has ever
                    been.</p>
                <p>The only notable public event that has occurred during my absence is the
                    reestablishment of friendly relations between the governments of Nicaragua and
                    Costa Rica.</p>
                <p>I hope to be again at the seat of the legation by the first of October.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEO. WILLIAMSON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
